    88	### 1.3 Blind matter census (articles only; frozen before any send)
    89	
    90	1. **Sample.** For each scored week, 40 kept articles drawn by seed 20261117, or all of them when a week has fewer. Read with week reading A1's
    91	   brief (`../2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/brief-A1.md`), unchanged except for the file names.
    92	2. **Readers.** Codex (memories off, closed, D661) and Claude (closed command line), apart. Nine weeks × 2 families, in fresh sessions.
    93	3. **Merge.** One matter is one bet. Two entries are one matter only when the same outcome settles both and their answers cannot move apart.
    94	   Another year of a figure is another matter.
    95	   - Each family merges its own nine lists in one session.
    96	   - Two reconcilers, one per family, map every matter across the two families' lists (`same` or `none`). A matter is listed by both when
    97	     both reconcilers say `same`.
    98	   - A one-family matter goes to two third readers (one per family) with its articles. It joins the list when both say it is worth a question.
    99	4. **The company-figure flag.** Each family marks every listed matter `company figure` or not, using D505's own list: 营收、毛利率的指引、市场预期、实际业绩.
   100	   The flag is set when both say yes; a split is listed.
   101	5. **N** is every listed matter (D665). The flagged subgroup is printed beside the verdict with its own a, b and N, because by D505 the new
   102	   system does not open such questions. Alex sees how much of any gap that rule makes. (D431: the comparison is of the products as
   103	   configured.)
   104	6. **Frozen:** `matters.json`. Each entry holds the matter, an example question, its source articles, its first source day and its flag.
   105	7. **Too small at once:** if N < 20, the verdict is "too small" and goes to Alex before any paid send.
   106	
   107	### 1.4 The verdict rule (`verdict-rule.json`, hashed; `score_comparison.py` with a test and a mutant per rule)
   108	
   109	1. **The cutoff:** one for every matter, the end of the invocation's last day (09-20 in the full run). Libraries are read as they stand then.
   110	   - Closed questions still count.
   111	   - Timeliness is not scored. The days from first source to opening are printed beside.
   112	2. **Covers (mapping, A2's form, blind):**
   113	   - **The rule:** both families mark the question `opened`, or `narrower` with both saying it carries the matter's main outcome
   114	     (ACCEPTANCE-DESIGN A; `tally_week.covered`). `broader` and `missed` do not cover.
   115	   - **Blinding:** the readers see each draw's library under a random X/Y label, the key outside the repository, and are told to ignore
   116	     style.
   117	   - **Disagreements:** two third readers, one per family. A remaining split is a split.
   118	3. **Same question:**
   119	   - **A question** is an id in a system-draw's library at the cutoff, the system's own identity. Distinct questions are distinct ids.
   120	   - **Matter to question:** the mapping rule above.
   121	   - **Question to question:** two questions of one library are the same bet when the same outcome settles both and their answers cannot move
   122	     apart (A1's rule; D248). Of such a pair, **the earlier-opened question is the original and the later one the duplicate** (as
   123	     `week/brief-A2.md` named the order).
   124	   - **During mapping:** the readers, who read each whole library, also mark its duplicates.
   125	   - **Distinct bets** are the ids less the duplicates both families mark. The 30-question minimum counts distinct bets. A split duplicate is
   126	     counted both ways, and the minimum must hold both ways.
   127	   - **On cards:** a duplicate stays a question in the worth guardrail.
   128	4. **The cards and their marks:**
   129	   - **Worth tracking:** worth, trivia or duplicate. Each card lists its library's other questions in opening order, with their opening days.
   130	     `duplicate` is marked only on the later question of a same-bet pair.
   131	   - **Answer:** supported, unsupported, insufficient, or no answer (a question not judged by the cutoff).
   132	   - **Cannot tell:** a split after the debate rounds.
   133	5. **Served in a draw:** at least one covering question is marked `worth` and supported. A matter **served by a system** is served in
   134	   at least 2 of its 3 draws.
   135	6. **The quantities:** a = served only by the new system; b = served only by the old; N = all listed matters.
   136	7. **The two countings:** every split resolved for the new system, or every split for the old.
   137	   - In each counting, a split mark becomes the decided mark it is resolved to, and enters the denominators.
   138	   - A guardrail subgroup is **exercised** only with at least 10 cards decided **without** a split.
   139	8. **The guardrails** (D428's measure, which D665 turns into these guardrails):
   140	   - **The sample:** the random guardrail sample only, 35 questions per system-draw by seed 20261117, or all of them when a library holds
   141	     fewer. Covering cards are not in these shares. The three draws are pooled per system.
   142	   - **The shares:**
   143	     - the share of **decided** figure-question answers (supported or unsupported) that are unsupported;
   144	     - the same for yes/no questions;
   145	     - the share of decided worth marks that are trivia or duplicate.
   146	   - **Reported beside, not in the shares:** insufficient, no answer and cannot tell.
   147	   - **The bar:** the new system's share may not be more than 10 points above the old system's.
   148	   - **Exercised:** each subgroup needs at least 10 cards per system decided without a split (item 7), or it is "not exercised".
   149	9. **The controls,** outside every product count:
   150	   - 6 planted wrong answers (a rung moved, 3 per system). Each needs an explicit unsupported or insufficient mark, or the reading is invalid.
   151	   - 4 supported controls, confirmed first by a separate reader.
   152	   - 3 hidden repeats.
   153	10. **No release verdict without six complete draws.** A draw that cannot finish (stopped after D449's one redo, or by its guard) gives no
   154	    release verdict by itself.
   155	    - **One replacement draw per system at most,** and only when:
   156	      - the stop's cause is not the system's own behaviour (a dropped network, the machine, or a launcher fault);
   157	      - the same manifest, window and guard are used;
   158	      - a council decision is recorded before its launch.
   159	    - The replacement takes the stopped draw's place. The guards then total at most $360.
   160	    - A stop caused by the system itself, or a second stop: what finished goes to Alex as a diagnostic, with the cause.
   161	11. **The verdicts, tested in this order:**
   162	    1. **Invalid:** a plant without an explicit rejection, fewer than 3 of 4 supported controls accepted, or fewer than 2 of 3 repeats marked
   163	       the same.
   164	    2. **Too small:** N < 20, or fewer than 30 distinct questions in any system-draw.
   165	    3. **Practical lead:** a − b ≥ max(3, ⌈0.15·N⌉) and a ≥ 2b under both countings, with every guardrail exercised and passing under both.
   166	    4. **Practical loss:** the mirror under both countings, or a guardrail breach under both.
   167	    5. **No practical winner:** everything else.
   168	12. **Printed beside, not deciding:**
   169	    - D428's limits next to the guardrails;
   170	    - a sign test and an interval, labelled illustrative;
   171	    - the company-figure subgroup;
   172	    - the floors (§1.6).
